<div class="byblos-docs-article">

    <h1>{{#str}}docs_topic_best_practices, local_byblos{{/str}}</h1>
    <p class="byblos-docs-lead">
        A strong portfolio is curated, reflective and coherent, not just a complete archive of everything
        you have done. These ten high-level practices come straight from the
        <a href="{{{links.pedagogy}}}">pedagogy</a> behind ePortfolios; each one notes how to apply it in Byblos.
    </p>

    <ol class="byblos-docs-steps byblos-docs-bp">
        <li>
            <strong>Start with purpose and audience.</strong>
            Decide first whether this portfolio is for <em>learning</em> (a reflective workspace), for
            <em>assessment</em> (graded against criteria), or a <em>showcase</em> (for an employer), the three pull in different directions, and being clear up front keeps the portfolio focused
            (Smith and Tillema, 2006; Barrett, 2010). Then design for the person who will read it.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Curate; don't accumulate.</strong>
            Choose your strongest, most representative evidence rather than uploading everything. A few
            well-chosen "signature" artefacts say more than a full archive. In Byblos, keep working
            material as <a href="{{{links.building_pages}}}">artefacts</a> and surface only the best on
            your pages.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Make reflection the engine.</strong>
            Reflection is where the learning happens, so caption every artefact. A simple, well-known
            prompt is <strong>"What? So what? Now what?"</strong>: what is this, what did I learn from it,
            and what will I do next. Add a Text section beside each piece of evidence to answer it
            (Yancey, 2009).
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Show growth, not just polish.</strong>
            Evidence of <em>change over time</em>, a rough first attempt next to a later one, often
            demonstrates more learning than a single finished product. Use a Timeline section or a
            sequence of pages to make progress visible.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Integrate, tell one story.</strong>
            Connect the dots across courses and experiences instead of presenting disconnected items.
            Gather related pages into a <a href="{{{links.collections}}}">collection</a> and use a home
            page to frame the throughline that ties everything together.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Align evidence to outcomes.</strong>
            If you are working towards programme outcomes, course criteria or a rubric, map your evidence
            to them explicitly so a reader can see the match. This "constructive alignment" is what makes a
            portfolio legible to an assessor (Wolf et al., 2022).
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Seek feedback and revise.</strong>
            Portfolios improve through dialogue. <a href="{{{links.sharing}}}">Share</a> a draft with a
            peer or teacher, or use peer review where a course offers it, and treat the portfolio as
            something you iterate, frequent, constructive feedback is one of the conditions that makes the
            practice high-impact (Kuh and O'Donnell, 2013).
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Design for your reader.</strong>
            Keep navigation clear and the look consistent, write concise headings, and make it accessible, meaningful alt text on images, real headings, sufficient contrast. A reader should be able to
            find the substance in seconds.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Demonstrate to a real audience.</strong>
            Public demonstration deepens the work. Match your audience to the right
            <a href="{{{links.sharing}}}">share</a>: an individual teacher, a whole course or cohort, or, for people outside Moodle such as an employer, a public link.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Mind privacy and integrity.</strong>
            Share deliberately and treat a public link like a password, anyone who has it can view the
            page, and you can revoke it at any time. Present honest, authentic work that is your own, and
            be careful with evidence that names or shows other people.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Keep it living.</strong>
            A portfolio is never really "finished", revisit it, retire stale items, and add new evidence
            as you grow. Because a published page keeps the same address, you can keep improving it and any
            link you have shared always shows the latest version.
        </li>
    </ol>

    <div class="byblos-docs-tip">
        <strong>A quick test before you share.</strong> Read your portfolio as the intended reader would.
        Can they tell, within a minute, who you are, what you can do, and what you have learned, and does
        every artefact earn its place with a reflection that explains why it is there? If not, curate and
        caption before you publish.
    </div>

    <div class="byblos-docs-note">
        <strong>Where these come from.</strong> Each practice is grounded in published research on
        ePortfolio pedagogy and high-impact practice. See <a href="{{{links.pedagogy}}}">Why ePortfolios
        work</a> for the concepts and the full reference list.
    </div>
</div>
